<h2>Three pages can carry a whole Michigan small business</h2>
<p>Simple Website at Webstar is three pages on purpose. Most owners do not need twenty menu items — they need clarity: what you do, why you are trustworthy, and how to hire you. I have built three-page sites for guides, trades, and retailers across Metro Detroit that rank and convert because each page has one job. This outline is what I ask you to approve in intake before design starts.</p>

<h2>Page 1 — Home: trust in ten seconds</h2>
<p>Lead with a headline that states outcome, not fluff. “Licensed HVAC repair in Warren and Sterling Heights” beats “Welcome to our website.” Follow with one short paragraph on who you serve and what makes you different — family-owned, emergency hours, bilingual staff, whatever is true.</p>
<p>Above the fold on mobile: click-to-call, hours or “by appointment,” and one hero photo of your real work. Social proof can be a short quote you approve, years in business, or license numbers — not fabricated star counts. Link clearly to Services and Contact.</p>

<h2>Page 1 sections that belong below the fold</h2>
<p>Add a scannable list of top services with links to anchors on the Services page if needed. Mention primary cities — Troy, Southfield, Detroit — only where accurate. A small “How it works” trio (call → schedule → done) reduces anxiety for first-time buyers.</p>
<p>Skip long company history on the homepage; save story for About blurb or Services intro. Keep file size light — compress photos before sending them to me.</p>

<h2>Page 2 — Services: specificity sells</h2>
<p>Break services into sections with plain headings: “Drain cleaning,” “Water heater replacement,” not internal jargon. Each section gets two to four sentences on what is included, typical timing, and whether you quote on-site. If you do not publish prices, say “free estimate” honestly.</p>
<p>Use bullets for boundaries: “We do not handle commercial refrigeration” prevents bad leads. For salons, list cut types and policy on deposits. For guides, list species, season, and gear provided vs what clients bring — Michigan regulations belong here when relevant.</p>

<h2>What not to cram onto Services</h2>
<p>Blog archives, full staff CVs, and photo galleries belong on future pages when you upgrade to Business Website. On three pages, link out to Instagram if that is where fresh photos live, but mirror the best shots on-site so Google sees them.</p>

<h2>Page 3 — Contact: remove friction</h2>
<p>Phone number as tap-to-call, email you monitor, and a short form (name, phone, message, optional service dropdown). State response time — “same business day” — and service area one more time. Embed hours; sync with Google Business Profile.</p>
<p>If you use booking software, one button to Calendly or similar is fine on Simple Website when scope allows; heavier booking integration often moves you to Business Website tier. Map embed only for storefronts with public parking notes if helpful.</p>

<h2>Content every three-page site still needs off-page</h2>
<p>Google Business Profile, Analytics, and Search Console — included on Webstar packages — carry reviews, photos, and performance data. Your three pages focus on conversion; the profile captures map traffic.</p>

<h2>Photos and copy you should gather before intake</h2>
<ul>
<li>Logo in PNG or SVG if possible</li>
<li>Six to twelve honest job or product photos</li>
<li>List of services with yes/no on what you refuse</li>
<li>Primary city list and phone tree (who answers?)</li>
<li>Existing domain or desired domain name</li>
</ul>

<h2>Accessibility and mobile checks</h2>
<p>Headings should step down logically (one h1 on home, h2 sections inside pages). Form fields need labels screen readers can announce. Contrast should pass for outdoor viewing — contractors often check sites in bright snow glare.</p>

<h2>When three pages is enough vs when to upgrade</h2>
<p>Three pages is enough when one crew, one location logic, and phone quotes drive revenue. Upgrade toward Business Website ($900–$1,800) when you need separate landing pages for commercial work, multiple stylists with booking, or brand-matched print for events.</p>

<h2>Launch week habits</h2>
<p>Test form deliverability — submit yourself from a friend’s phone. Click every button. Update GBP website field. Share the new link on the Facebook page you already have, but make the site the canonical home for hours and services.</p>
<p>This structure keeps builds inside $350–$500 for most Michigan clients and launches in about 24 hours when assets arrive ready. Submit intake with your three-page outline and we confirm before pixels hit the screen.</p>
<h2>Homepage hero mistakes I see in Michigan</h2>
<p>Rotating sliders with five stock photos slow phones and say nothing. Single strong photo of your team, boat, salon chair, or completed deck wins. Headlines written for humans beat keyword soup. If you serve multiple counties, say so once clearly — not in a footer-only list invisible to scanners.</p>

<h2>Writing services without a copywriter</h2>
<p>Record a voice memo explaining what you do, send it with intake, and I shape it into scannable sections. Bullet lists beat walls of text for HVAC and cleaning. Mention licenses, insurance, and warranties where they differentiate you from fly-by-night competitors on Craigslist.</p>

<h2>Trust signals that actually matter locally</h2>
<p>BBB or chamber logos only if you maintain membership. Association badges for trades. Photos of branded trucks on Michigan roads. Link to live Google reviews once you have them — never fake aggregate ratings. About blurb can live on Home or Services when three pages constrain you.</p>

<h2>Contact page extras that help conversion</h2>
<p>Repeat phone at top and bottom. Note if you text confirmations. Parking instructions for Main Street Royal Oak storefronts reduce no-shows. Service-area businesses clarify travel fees before the form submit.</p>

<h2>Planning page two growth without a rebuild</h2>
<p>When revenue justifies Business Website, we add pages without throwing away design tokens — colors, typography, button styles carry forward. Planning headings on Services that could become future slugs (/commercial-hvac/) makes upgrades cheaper.</p>

<h2>Examples of three-page sitemaps by trade</h2>
<ul>
<li>Guide: Home, Trips &amp; rates, Contact</li>
<li>Plumber: Home, Services, Contact</li>
<li>Salon: Home, Services &amp; pricing approach, Contact</li>
<li>Cleaner: Home, Services &amp; checklist, Contact</li>
</ul>
<p>Each maps cleanly to Simple Website scope at $350–$500 with Google setup included.</p>
<h2>SEO basics without bloating page count</h2>
<p>One strong title tag and meta description per page, headings that mirror how customers talk, and internal links between Home, Services, and Contact are enough for many local businesses. City names belong where accurate — not stuffed into hidden divs. Blog posts can wait until Business Website scope.</p>
<p>Schema for local business can be added on custom builds so Google understands phone and hours. Pair that with an accurate profile rather than chasing vanity keywords across fifteen landings you will never maintain.</p>

<h2>Maintenance rhythm on three pages</h2>
<p>Quarterly photo refresh, hour updates before holidays, and a quick check that form email still delivers — that is ongoing ops. Care Plans cover edits if you prefer not to touch HTML. WaaS at $150 per month bundles those edits during the minimum term.</p>
<h2>Quick recap for busy owners</h2>
<p>You do not need a marketing degree to execute this — you need a consistent process tied to jobs you already finish. Block thirty minutes on your calendar after launch week to walk the steps above with whoever answers your phone. Revisit the list each quarter when Michigan seasons change demand, hours, and photo opportunities. If anything here overlaps with paid packages, it is because Webstar builds sites and Google setup together so you are not juggling five vendors with conflicting advice.</p>
